A||||6||Gold87SE||Tape Player Belt||||1||
Z||000000||Gold87SE||10-06-2000||12:09 AM||||I bought a new belt for my tape player but I know absolutely nothing about tape players. I don't even know where the original belt is located in the tape player. How do I install this new belt?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||10-06-2000||01:33 AM||||You need to open up radio and remove the tape player. Belt is located on the bottom.<BR>It's kind of a pain to get the cassette player out. There are 4 screws and 2 electrical connectors to disconnect. Then wiggle it out. Most car stereo service centers will do it for $35.00.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ROB MAC||10-07-2000||11:45 AM||||Also the belt may be broken and you may need to figure out the proper way to install the new one. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-07-2000||12:29 PM||||Just wondering, I should know this, but I dont. When the belt on the tape playing breaks, what will the player do? Will it just not play the tape, or will it reject the tape all together? When I got my car, the tape player would not accept a tape, I would push it in, and it would click for a sec, and push it back out. Is this a broken belt? I didnt really bother with it back then because I already knew I would put a CD player in.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||ROB MAC||10-08-2000||01:11 PM||||If your player is the type where the tape goes in manually then the tape will just stop when the belt breaks. If your player pulls the tape in like a VCR then you may have other cause than a broken belt. Hard to tell without pulling apart.||||reg||1
